## The idea

Start with **n separate groups**. Every edge that connects two **different** groups merges them, so the count drops by one. Union-find tells you quickly whether two nodes are already in the same group.

## Step by step

1. groups = n; every node is its own parent.
2. For each edge: find both roots.
3. Different roots → join them, groups--.
4. Same root → nothing (a cycle or a duplicate edge).

## Worked example

**Input:** n = 5, edges 0-1, 1-2, 3-4

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| 0-1 | join → 4 groups |
| 1-2 | join → 3 |
| 3-4 | join → 2 |

**Result:** 2

## Cost

O((n + e) · α(n)) — practically linear.

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
// LeetCode 323 — how many connected groups do n nodes and these undirected edges form?
public static class Components {
    public static int Count(int n, int[][] edges) {
        var parent = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) parent[i] = i;
        int groups = n;                        // start with every node alone
        foreach (var e in edges ?? new int[0][]) {
            int a = Root(parent, e[0]), b = Root(parent, e[1]);
            if (a != b) { parent[a] = b; groups--; }   // each edge that joins two groups removes one
        }
        return groups;
    }

    private static int Root(int[] parent, int x) {
        while (parent[x] != x) { parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]; x = parent[x]; }
        return x;
    }
}
```

## Likely follow-up questions

**Q: Cycles and duplicate edges?**

They join nodes already in one group, so the count does not change.

**Q: Complexity?**

Near-linear with path compression.

**Q: BFS instead?**

Also O(n + e); start a new BFS from every unvisited node and count the starts.

**Q: Edges added live, queries interleaved.**

Union-find handles adds; deletes are much harder (offline tricks or a dynamic-connectivity structure).

